  "answer": "Not necessarily prohibited, but consent can be important. I cannot determine whether your particular application would be registered.\n\n**Act — section 43:** The supplied provision does not impose a blanket consent requirement for famous names. It requires rejection where a connotation of the mark would make its use for the specified goods or services likely to deceive or cause confusion [TMA1995/s43].\n\n**Manual practice:** A famous name may attract a section 43 objection where consumers are likely to assume that the person is connected with, endorses, produces or supplies the goods or services. If permission from the person or their representative is clear, the Manual says that this section 43 ground does not arise; if the examiner is not satisfied that the relevant relationship exists, an objection may be appropriate [TMM/Part29/6#1~1]. Thus, absence of consent is not presented as an automatic bar, but a misleading implication of endorsement is a concern.\n\nThe examiner considers how well known the person was among relevant Australian consumers at the priority date, whether consumers would identify the name with that particular person, and the connection between the goods/services and their field of fame. Goods/services outside that field are not necessarily free of concern: consumers may expect high-profile celebrities to endorse unrelated products [TMM/Part29/6#1~2].\n\nThese passages address section 43 only; they do not establish that an application satisfying this test meets every requirement for registration."
